const body = `<h1>5.2b — Overloaded Constructors: Multiple Ways to Build</h1>

<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Basic Application</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~20 min</span>
    </div>

    <div class="section">
        <h3>Build: Weapon with Defaults</h3>
        <p>Sometimes you want to create an object with full detail; other times you want a quick default version. Overloaded constructors give you both.</p>
        <p>Create a class called <code>Weapon</code>:</p>
        <ol>
            <li>Instance variables: <code>String name</code>, <code>int damage</code>, <code>String damageType</code>.</li>
            <li>Constructor 1: takes <code>name</code> only. Sets damage = 5, damageType = "physical".</li>
            <li>Constructor 2: takes <code>name</code> and <code>damage</code>. Sets damageType = "physical".</li>
            <li>Constructor 3: takes all three values.</li>
            <li>IMPORTANT: Each constructor should call the most detailed one using <code>this(name, damage, "physical")</code> or similar. Don't duplicate assignment code.</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="section">
        <h3>Test</h3>
        <p>Create weapons using all three constructors. What happens if you only provide a constructor with all 3 params but try to call <code>new Weapon("Sword")</code>? Why doesn't Java just use defaults?</p>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="resources">
    <h3>Reference Docs</h3>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-05/constructors.html">Constructors</a></li>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-05/class-design.html">Writing Classes</a></li>
    </ul>
</div>`;
